# Tax or quota?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "When does it matter whether we fix the price or the quantity of pollution? What did Weitzman show about uncertainty and the choice?"
next: {"title":"Why won't the market build a lighthouse?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/46-why-won-t-the-market-build-a-lighthouse.cast"}

## A price that missed
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Expected
      - Early 1996
    values:
      - 300
      - 70
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 350
    y_label: Permit price ($ per ton of SO₂)
    x_label: US sulphur permits
    gap: 0.6
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 110
      w: 620
      h: 540
sources:
    - id: montero
      title: 'Explaining low sulfur dioxide allowance prices: the effect of expectation errors and irreversibility'
      authors: Montero, J.-P. & Ellerman, A. D.
      year: 1998
      finding: Early studies put the marginal cost of compliance with the 1990 Acid Rain Program (Title IV) at about $300 per ton (EPRI 1993 forecast $273); EPA's first auction in March 1993 cleared at $131 and prices sank to a low slightly below $70 in early 1996.
      url: https://ceepr.mit.edu/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/98011.pdf
    - id: weitzman
      title: Prices vs. Quantities
      authors: Weitzman, M. L.
      year: 1974
      finding: Under uncertainty about costs, a price instrument beats a quantity instrument when the marginal-cost curve is steeper than the marginal-benefit (damage) curve, and vice versa.

    text cue_cap "1990: a cap on SO₂" x 830 y 560 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "A price that missed"

When we want less pollution, does it matter whether we fix its price or its amount? Start with America's power plants in 1990.
    draw axes

B: Their sulphur smoke was falling as acid rain. So Congress (@draw cue_cap@) capped it: a fixed number of tons a year, with permits that firms could buy and sell.

A permit's price should match what it costs to cut the last ton. Earl (@draw bar_1@)y studies put that at about three hundred dollars a ton.

    ask "Drag the early-1996 bar to your guess." on bar_2 store g default "200" voice b
        intro: So guess. When the market got going, what did a permit cost by early 1996?
        right: Well judged: you said {g} dollars; it was about {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g} dollars; it was about {g.true}.

Just under seventy dollars. Cleaning up had turned out far cheaper than anyone thought.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

B: Isn't that just good news?
    highlight bar_2

    erase cue_cap

Good news, but look where the surprise went. The cap fixed the tons,
    text cue_q "Cap: tons fixed" #2f6b8f x 830 y 500 font_size 36

so the whole error landed on the price.
    text cue_p "→ price surprise" #2f6b8f x 830 y 450 font_size 36

A tax of three hundred dollars would have done the opposite: the price fixed,
    text cue_t "Tax: price fixed" #b5482e x 830 y 350 font_size 36

and firms cutting far more than planned.
    text cue_t2 "→ tons surprise" #b5482e x 830 y 300 font_size 36

B: So which mistake would you rather make?
    highlight cue_q cue_t

    erase cue_q cue_p cue_t cue_t2

In 1974 the economist Martin Weitzman showed that the answer depends on two slopes,
    text cue_w "Martin Weitzman, 1974:\n'Prices vs. Quantities'" #8a5fa8 cites ["weitzman"] x 830 y 450 font_size 34

and that it only matters at all because nobody knows the costs in advance.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

    pause 0.4

## Two levers, one surprise
domain: {"x":[0,105],"y":[0,110]}
vars:
    th:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 20
    sd:
      value: 0.8
      min: 0.4
      max: 2
    sc:
      value: 0.8
      min: 0.6
      max: 2
    xq:
      expr: 80/(sd+sc)
    tx:
      expr: 10+sd*80/(sd+sc)
    xo:
      expr: (80+th)/(sd+sc)
    xt:
      expr: 80/(sd+sc)+th/sc
    pq:
      expr: 90+th-sc*80/(sd+sc)
    Lt:
      expr: th*th*sd*sd/(2*sc*sc*(sd+sc))
    Lq:
      expr: th*th/(2*(sd+sc))

    axes ax x_label "Soot (t/year)" y_label "$ per tonne" cross corner ticks.x [25,50] ticks.y [25,50,75,100] hidden true
    curve mac_exp dashed thick #2f6b8f expr "90 - sc*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 style.opacity 0.3 hidden true
    curve mac #2f6b8f expr "90 + th - sc*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label l_mac "MAC: cost of cutting" above #2f6b8f attach_to mac font_size 26 hidden true
    curve md #b5482e expr "10 + sd*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label l_md "MD: harm" above-left #b5482e attach_to md font_size 26 hidden true
    curve taxline dashed thick #d0865f expr tx x_from 0 x_to 100 hidden true
    label l_tax "tax ${tx:0}" right #d0865f attach_to taxline font_size 26 hidden true
    curve quotaline dashed thick #8a5fa8 x_expr xq y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 104 hidden true
    label l_quota "quota {xq:0} t" above #8a5fa8 attach_to quotaline font_size 26 hidden true
    dot E #3d3833 at.on md at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xq"} hidden true
    text l_E "E: 50 t, $50" at.data [64,50] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot O #3d3833 at.on md at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xo"} hidden true
    label l_O "best {xo:0} t" above attach_to O font_size 26 hidden true
    dot Pt #d0865f at.on mac at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xt"} hidden true
    label l_Pt "tax: {xt:0} t" above-right #d0865f attach_to Pt font_size 26 hidden true
    dot Pq #8a5fa8 at.on mac at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xq"} hidden true
    label l_Pq "quota: ${pq:0}" left #8a5fa8 attach_to Pq font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Two levers, one surprise"

B: Let's shrink it to Harbourtown. The boatyard puts soot over the harbour, with new illustrative numbers.
    draw ax

Across, tonnes of soot a yea (@draw mac l_mac@)r. This blue line is what it costs the yard to cut the last tonne: cheap at first, dearer the cleaner it gets.

The red line is the harm each extra tonne does to the town.
    draw md l_md

The best amount is where they cross: fifty tonnes, at fifty dollars a tonne.
    draw E l_E

    pause 0.3

    erase l_E

The council can get there two ways: tax every tonne at fifty dollars,
    draw taxline l_tax

or hand out permits for fifty tonnes.
    draw quotaline l_quota

B: And with the costs known, both land on the same point.
    highlight E effect pulse

Exactly. But the council guesses the blue line. Suppose cleaning up turns out dearer than it thought.
    highlight mac

    show mac_exp

The true cost line sits higher,
    animate th 16 duration 2.5

and the best amount is now sixty tonnes.
    draw O l_O

    quiz "Costs turned out higher. Under the $50 tax, what happens to the boatyard's soot?" intro "Guess first." judge false
        right: It rises: at fifty dollars a tonne, cutting is now dearer than paying, so the yard cuts less.
        * It rises
        * It stays at 50 tonnes
        * It falls

    camera center.data [60,48] zoom 1.2 duration 1.2

The tax holds the price at fifty, so the yard cuts less and emits seventy: ten tonnes too many.
    draw Pt l_Pt

That orange triangle is the harm of those extra tonnes, beyond what cutting them would have cost.
    region loss_t x_from.ref O x_to.ref Pt between ["md","mac"] style.fill #d0865f style.opacity 0.75

The quota holds fifty tonnes, so the permit price climbs to sixty-six dollars.
    draw Pq l_Pq

B: And now the loss is cutting ten tonnes that were worth more to the yard than the harm they'd have done.
    region loss_q x_from.ref E x_to.ref O between ["mac","md"] style.fill #8a5fa8 style.opacity 0.7

Two instruments, two errors: the tax misses on tonnes, the quota on cost. Here the triangles happen to be the same size.
    highlight loss_t loss_q

    camera reset true duration 1

    pause 0.4

## Which triangle is bigger?
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 105
    'y':
      - 0
      - 110
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 700
      h: 510
vars:
    th:
      value: 16
      min: 0
      max: 20
    sd:
      value: 0.8
      min: 0.4
      max: 2
    sc:
      value: 0.8
      min: 0.6
      max: 2
    xq:
      expr: 80/(sd+sc)
    tx:
      expr: 10+sd*80/(sd+sc)
    xo:
      expr: (80+th)/(sd+sc)
    xt:
      expr: 80/(sd+sc)+th/sc
    pq:
      expr: 90+th-sc*80/(sd+sc)
    Lt:
      expr: th*th*sd*sd/(2*sc*sc*(sd+sc))
    Lq:
      expr: th*th/(2*(sd+sc))
    wt:
      expr: max(0,min(1,(Lq-Lt)*0.1))
    wq:
      expr: max(0,min(1,(Lt-Lq)*0.1))

    axes ax x_label "Soot (t/year)" y_label "$ per tonne" cross corner ticks.x [25,50] ticks.y [25,50,75,100] hidden true
    curve mac_exp dashed thick #2f6b8f expr "90 - sc*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 style.opacity 0.3 hidden true
    curve mac #2f6b8f expr "90 + th - sc*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label l_mac "MAC: cost of cutting" above #2f6b8f attach_to mac font_size 26 hidden true
    curve md #b5482e expr "10 + sd*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label l_md "MD: harm" above-left #b5482e attach_to md font_size 26 hidden true
    curve taxline dashed thick #d0865f expr tx x_from 0 x_to 100 hidden true
    label l_tax "tax ${tx:0}" right #d0865f attach_to taxline font_size 26 hidden true
    curve quotaline dashed thick #8a5fa8 x_expr xq y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 104 hidden true
    label l_quota "quota {xq:0} t" above #8a5fa8 attach_to quotaline font_size 26 hidden true
    dot E #3d3833 at.on md at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xq"} hidden true
    dot O #3d3833 at.on md at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xo"} hidden true
    dot Pt #d0865f at.on mac at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xt"} hidden true
    dot Pq #8a5fa8 at.on mac at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xq"} hidden true
    region loss_t x_from.ref O x_to.ref Pt between ["md","mac"] style.fill #d0865f style.opacity 0.75 hidden true
    region loss_q x_from.ref E x_to.ref O between ["mac","md"] style.fill #8a5fa8 style.opacity 0.7 hidden true
    text r_t "tax loss ${Lt:0}" #d0865f x 850 y 520 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r_q "quota loss ${Lq:0}" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 465 font_size 34 hidden true
    text w_t "tax wins" #d0865f x 850 y 405 font_size 34 bind {"style.opacity":"wt"} style.opacity 0 hidden true
    text w_q "quota wins" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 405 font_size 34 bind {"style.opacity":"wq"} style.opacity 0 hidden true
    text rule "pin down the steeper line" x 850 y 330 font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mcard top-right work [{"tex":"\\text{slopes: harm } d,\\ \\text{cost } c;\\ \\text{surprise } \\theta"},{"tex":"L_{quota} = \\theta^2 / (2(d+c))"},{"tex":"L_{tax} = L_{quota} \\cdot (d/c)^2"},{"tex":"\\text{tax wins} \\iff d < c"}] font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Which triangle is bigger?"

The triangles weren't equal by luck.
    draw ax mac_exp mac l_mac md l_md taxline l_tax quotaline l_quota E O Pt Pq loss_t loss_q

Here both lines have the same slope.
    draw r_t r_q w_t w_q

B: Then make the harm steep. Say each extra tonne hurts much more than the one before.
    animate sd 1.6 duration 2.5

The tax's mistake was in tonnes, and with steep harm every wrong tonne costs a lot. Its triangle swells; the quota's shrinks.
    highlight loss_t r_t

So when harm turns sharply, fix the quantity.
    highlight quotaline w_q

B: And the other way round? If the cost of cutting is the steep line,
    animate sd 0.8 sc 1.6 duration 2.5

B: a wrong tonne is cheap, and forcing the yard to an exact number is what hurts.
    highlight loss_q r_q

Then fix the price. That's Weitzman's rule: the steeper line tells you what to pin down.
    draw rule

Your turn: pull the surprise to zero, then tilt either line, and watch which loss is smaller.
    explore params ["th","sd","sc"]

    animate th 16 sd 0.8 sc 1.6 duration 1

B: With no surprise, both triangles vanish. So uncertainty is the whole reason the choice matters.
    animate th 0 duration 2.5

And the size of the surprise scales both triangles alike. It decides how much is at stake; the slopes decide who wins.
    animate th 20 duration 3

    erase rule

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own store m default "Skip it"

@math

    erase mathq

    hide r_t r_q w_t w_q

Call the harm line's slope d, the cost line's slope c, and the cost surprise theta.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

The quota's triangle is theta squared, over two times d plus c.
    draw mcard_line_2

The tax's is the same, times d over c, squared.
    draw mcard_line_3

So the tax wins exactly when d is smaller than c, and theta only sets the stakes.
    draw mcard_line_4

    erase mcard

@test

    erase mathq

    show r_t r_q w_t w_q

    pause 0.3

## Carbon, cliffs and hybrids
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Weitzman would sign that.
    good: Most of the slope-reading done; the cliffs are the hard part.
    poor: Look at which line is steep, then pin that one down.
    none: 'Prices or quantities: it was never a matter of taste.'
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 105
    'y':
      - 0
      - 110
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 700
      h: 510
vars:
    th:
      value: 16
      min: 0
      max: 20
    sd:
      value: 0.4
      min: 0.4
      max: 2
    sc:
      value: 1.6
      min: 0.6
      max: 2
    xq:
      expr: 80/(sd+sc)
    tx:
      expr: 10+sd*80/(sd+sc)
    xo:
      expr: (80+th)/(sd+sc)
    xt:
      expr: 80/(sd+sc)+th/sc
    pq:
      expr: 90+th-sc*80/(sd+sc)
    Lt:
      expr: th*th*sd*sd/(2*sc*sc*(sd+sc))
    Lq:
      expr: th*th/(2*(sd+sc))
    wt:
