sources:
    - id: pizer
      title: Combining price and quantity controls to mitigate global climate change
      authors: Pizer, W. A.
      year: 2002
      finding: In a stochastic CGE model with uncertain compliance costs, the expected welfare gain from the optimal carbon price policy is about five times that from the optimal quantity policy; a hybrid (permits plus a trigger price) does better still.
      doi: 10.1016/S0047-2727(01)00118-9
    - id: weitzman
      title: Prices vs. Quantities
      authors: Weitzman, M. L.
      year: 1974
      finding: Under cost uncertainty, prices beat quantities when the marginal-cost curve is steeper than the marginal-benefit (damage) curve, and vice versa.

    axes ax x_label "Emissions (t/year)" y_label "$ per tonne" cross corner ticks.x [25,50] ticks.y [25,50,75,100] hidden true
    curve mac_exp dashed thick #2f6b8f expr "90 - sc*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 style.opacity 0.3 hidden true
    curve mac #2f6b8f expr "90 + th - sc*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label l_mac "MAC: cost of cutting" above #2f6b8f attach_to mac font_size 26 hidden true
    curve md #b5482e expr "10 + sd*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label l_md "MD: harm" above-left #b5482e attach_to md font_size 26 hidden true
    curve taxline dashed thick #d0865f expr tx x_from 0 x_to 100 hidden true
    label l_tax "tax ${tx:0}" right #d0865f attach_to taxline font_size 26 hidden true
    curve quotaline dashed thick #8a5fa8 x_expr xq y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 104 hidden true
    label l_quota "quota {xq:0} t" above #8a5fa8 attach_to quotaline font_size 26 hidden true
    dot E #3d3833 at.on md at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xq"} hidden true
    dot O #3d3833 at.on md at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xo"} hidden true
    dot Pt #d0865f at.on mac at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xt"} hidden true
    dot Pq #8a5fa8 at.on mac at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"xq"} hidden true
    region loss_t x_from.ref O x_to.ref Pt between ["md","mac"] style.fill #d0865f style.opacity 0.75 hidden true
    region loss_q x_from.ref E x_to.ref O between ["mac","md"] style.fill #8a5fa8 style.opacity 0.7 hidden true
    text r_t "tax loss ${Lt:0}" #d0865f x 850 y 560 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r_q "quota loss ${Lq:0}" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 505 font_size 34 hidden true
    text w_t "tax wins" #d0865f x 850 y 445 font_size 34 bind {"style.opacity":"wt"} style.opacity 0 hidden true
    text w_q "quota wins" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 445 font_size 34 bind {"style.opacity":"wq"} style.opacity 0 hidden true
    text c_co2 "CO₂: stock, not flow" #b5482e x 850 y 340 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_pizer "Pizer 2002: price ≈ 5× gain" cites ["pizer"] x 850 y 285 font_size 28 hidden true
    text c_cliff "tipping point?" #b5482e x 850 y 340 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_hyb "Hybrid: cap +" x 850 y 340 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_hyb2 "price floor & ceiling" x 850 y 300 font_size 30 hidden true
    text stamp "mostly agreed" #b5482e x 850 y 220 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Carbon, cliffs and hybrids"

Now carbon. Warming depends on all the carbon piled up over a century,
    draw ax mac_exp mac l_mac md l_md taxline l_tax quotaline l_quota E O Pt Pq loss_t loss_q r_t r_q w_t w_q

so one year's extra tonne barely changes the harm of the next.
    draw c_co2

B: A flat harm line. So the rule says: tax it.
    highlight md w_t

That's what the models find. William Pizer's  (@draw c_pizer@)2002 simulations put the expected gain from a carbon price at about five times that from a fixed quantity.

    erase c_co2 c_pizer

B: But what if there's a cliff? An ice sheet that goes past some level of warming?
    draw c_cliff

Then near the cliff the harm line turns much steeper, and the rule flips toward pinning the tonnes.
    animate sd 2 duration 3 ghost ["md"]

The clean answer only holds away from the edge.
    highlight w_q

    erase c_cliff md_ghost

B: And politicians like a cap because it promises the tonnes, whatever the price.
    highlight quotaline

Which is why some real schemes, California's among them, mix the two: a cap, with a floor and a ceiling on the permit price.
    draw c_hyb c_hyb2

How sure are we (@draw stamp@)? Weitzman's logic is mostly agreed.

Where the slopes really lie, for carbon near a tipping point, is still argued.
    highlight md

    erase c_hyb c_hyb2 stamp

    quiz "A chemical in a lake is harmless up to a level and kills the fish above it. Cleanup costs are uncertain. Tax or quota?" intro "Three new cases to try."
        right: A quota: the harm line is nearly vertical at the danger level, so pin the amount below it.
        wrong: Which line is steep near the danger level? The harm line: here, a quota.
        * A tax
        + A quota
        * It makes no difference

    hide ax mac_exp mac l_mac md l_md taxline l_tax quotaline l_quota E O Pt Pq loss_t loss_q r_t r_q w_t w_q

    cards sortc check each
        Tax: Harm grows slowly with stock
        Quota: Harm jumps past a threshold
        * "Cost uncertain, harm flat" bin Tax
        * "Flat cost, steep harm" bin Quota

    ask "Tax or quota? Sort each case." on sortc store f stage own voice b
        right: Threshold and steep harm go to quota, slow stock and flat harm to tax: steep harm pins the tonnes, steep cost or flat harm pins the price.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Quota: the threshold, the steep harm. Tax: the slow stock, the flat harm.

    erase sortc

    show ax mac_exp mac l_mac md l_md taxline l_tax quotaline l_quota E O Pt Pq loss_t loss_q r_t r_q w_t w_q

    quiz "A paper mill's cleanup costs are known to the cent, and so is the harm downstream. Tax or quota?"
        right: Either: with no surprise there are no triangles, and both reach the same point.
        wrong: What made the triangles appear in the first place? A surprise in costs, and here there is none: either works.
        * The tax
        * The quota
        + Either: they land on the same point

So does it matter whether we fix the price or the amount? Only because we can't see the costs in advance,
    highlight r_t r_q

and then the steeper line tells you what to fix: fixing a market failure is also choosing which mistake you can live with.
    highlight loss_t loss_q

    pause 0.5

## Where next
